# Kernel config fragment: NT-oriented Linux synchronization support.
#
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 7 ("Prefer Linux's NT-oriented
# synchronization facilities where available") and section 53 immediate
# target #3 both point at this: the `ntsync` misc-device driver, merged
# into mainline Linux in 6.14, implements NT event/mutex/semaphore wait
# semantics (including wait-all/wait-any) in-kernel, specifically so Wine
# doesn't have to emulate them with slow userspace polling/futex chains.
# This is the wait primitive `runtime/ntdll` and (later) `ntabi`/`ntd`
# should build on rather than reinventing (Rule 1).
#
# Merge this fragment into the kernel config used to build `linux` for the
# reference image (or confirm it's already set — Arch's stock `linux`
# package has shipped it enabled by default since it landed upstream).
# Verify on a built image with:
#   zgrep NTSYNC /proc/config.gz    # or: grep NTSYNC /boot/config-$(uname -r)
#   ls /dev/ntsync                  # device node should exist
#
# Not yet verified in this repo — no build environment in this sandbox to
# produce a kernel or boot a built image against. Tracked in ROADMAP.md
# Phase 0.

CONFIG_NTSYNC=y

# Virtualization/passthrough prerequisites for the Phase 4+ ReactOS driver
# cell (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md sections 14-27). Not required for Phase 0's
# baseline distro goal, but listed here since they're also kernel-config
# concerns and easy to enable once, rather than revisit later:
# CONFIG_KVM=y
# CONFIG_KVM_INTEL=m
# CONFIG_KVM_AMD=m
# CONFIG_VFIO=m
# CONFIG_VFIO_PCI=m
# CONFIG_VFIO_MDEV=m
# CONFIG_IOMMUFD=m
# CONFIG_IOMMU_SUPPORT=y
# CONFIG_INTEL_IOMMU=y
# CONFIG_AMD_IOMMU=y
